## Auto-behaviours (no payload flag needed)

| Behaviour | Trigger condition | What happens |
|---|---|---|
| Auto ID assignment | `id` field is empty or omitted | Assigned as `task1`, `task2`... sorted by `executor_order_id` ASC then `executor_sequence_id` ASC |
| Name deduplication | Same `name` in multiple layers | Airflow task_id becomes `name_order_seq` automatically |
| Merge node detection | A node appears as downstream of more than one parent | `TriggerRule.NONE_FAILED_MIN_ONE_SUCCESS` applied + merge guard XCom check added |
| Orphan detection | A node has no generated downstream business child | Auto-wired to `finalize_results` so the DAG terminates cleanly |

### Current Cross-Layer Wiring Rules

The implementation does not always make every node in layer N+1 wait for every node in layer N:

1. Every node in the first layer is wired from `run_started_event`.
2. When the next layer contains exactly one node, every non-branch node in the previous layer is wired to it.
3. When the next layer contains multiple nodes, a non-branch node is wired only to the next-layer node with the same `executor_sequence_id`.
4. Branch-source nodes do not receive structural outbound edges; their explicit success/failure targets define their outbound wiring.
5. A node with no generated downstream business edge is treated as terminal/orphan and wired to `finalize_results`.

Keep sequence IDs aligned deliberately. A multi-node next layer with unmatched sequence IDs can create tasks with no upstream dependency.

### Implementation Accuracy Notes

- `run_control_id` is sanitized and converted to `<sanitized_run_control_id>_dag`; it is not used verbatim as the Airflow DAG ID.
- `engine` is validated only as a non-empty string. The service does not currently enforce `"PYTHON"`.
- Nodes sharing an `executor_order_id` are in the same layer, but their actual upstream dependencies still depend on the cross-layer wiring rules above.
- A one-sided branch is accepted at build time, but the generated branch operator raises an `AirflowException` if the empty side is selected. Use both success and failure targets for runtime-safe branching.
- Branch recovery routes do not erase the original failed task state. A failed branch-source task can still make the final DAG result fail even when its failure route runs successfully.
- The current generated branch skip whitelist stores Airflow task names, while finalization compares internal node IDs. When `id` and `name` differ, an intentionally skipped branch can be reported as an unexpected skip and fail finalization.
- Node `name` is used directly as the Airflow `task_id`. Keep names Airflow-safe, unique after generated suffixing, and avoid generated task IDs such as `prepare_inputs`, `run_started_event`, `finalize_results`, `run_final_event`, and `completion`.

