      "on_success_node_ids": [],
      "on_failure_node_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "branch_c",
      "name": "branch_c",
      "executor_build_id": "BRANCH_C_API",
      "engine": "PYTHON",
      "executor_order_id": 2,
      "executor_sequence_id": 3,
      "execution_mode": "sync",
      "branch_on_status": false,
      "on_success_node_ids": [],
      "on_failure_node_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "handle_failure",
      "name": "handle_failure",
      "executor_build_id": "HANDLE_FAILURE_API",
      "engine": "PYTHON",
      "executor_order_id": 2,
      "executor_sequence_id": 4,
      "execution_mode": "sync",
      "branch_on_status": false,
      "on_success_node_ids": [],
      "on_failure_node_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "merge_results",
      "name": "merge_results",
      "executor_build_id": "MERGE_RESULTS_API",
      "engine": "PYTHON",
      "executor_order_id": 3,
      "executor_sequence_id": 1,
      "execution_mode": "sync",
      "branch_on_status": false,
      "on_success_node_ids": [],
      "on_failure_node_ids": []
    }
  ]
}
```

How it works:

1. `decision` has `branch_on_status: true`.
2. Its `on_success_node_ids` contains three node IDs, so `branch_a`, `branch_b`, and `branch_c` are selected together on success.
3. Those three nodes share `executor_order_id: 2`, so they can run in parallel.
4. `handle_failure` is also in layer 2, but it is selected only when `decision` fails.
5. `merge_results` is the only node in layer 3. The generator wires all four layer-2 nodes to it and applies `TriggerRule.NONE_FAILED_MIN_ONE_SUCCESS`.
6. Unselected branch tasks are skipped; the merge runs after the selected route completes.

> Keep `id` and `name` identical for branch targets until the current branch skip-whitelist mismatch is fixed. Also note that a failed `decision` task remains failed even when `handle_failure` succeeds, so final DAG status can still be failure.

